          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Living water primarily signifies God’s life-giving gift, especially the Holy Spirit, bringing salvation and eternal life through Christ; the word or gospel is a recognized minority extension.\",\"book_summary\":\"Living water signifies both God’s Spirit and God’s word proceeding outward to impart life.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The Holy Spirit as God’s life-giving saving gift.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The outgoing Spirit and word of God as joint life-giving agencies.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book elevates the word of God from a minority or secondary interpretation to part of the term’s central identification alongside the Spirit. It also emphasizes their outward, life-giving action rather than primarily the gift received by believers.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction because the book retains the consensus’s central identification with the Spirit. The difference is the material addition of God’s word as a co-referent.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the dominant Spirit identification but broadens the core to include the word of God. Since word/gospel imagery already appears among recognized alternatives, the reading is not novel. The change is substantive enough to be REFINED rather than MATCH because it promotes a secondary interpretation into the defining core.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"John 7:38, especially when interpreted by John 7:39, supports the Spirit, while Jeremiah 2:13 identifies God as the fountain of living waters. Neither printed citation directly identifies living water with God’s word, so the book’s added co-referent is not established by these references alone.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
